<!----></div><gg-markup-content _ngcontent-ng-c1392637832=3D"">Hi everyone!=
  <br><br>We've designed and thoroughly play-tested a variant of Bubastis t=
hat we find much more enjoyable than the 'vanilla' Bubastis (at least among=
st die-hard veteran players).  It gives them a completely different feel, a=
 much more challenging path to victory while still maintaining their overal=
l 'power level' and viability, and almost completely eliminates the annoyan=
ce of other players by having them in the game (their 'griefiness level'). =
 It does this by swapping 2 of their Spellbooks and having a minor errata c=
hange to the Moon.  <br><br>If you are playing with experienced players of =
CW, we feel that this variant of Bubastis is better in every respect.  If y=
ou're playing among newer, or less experienced players, then you may enjoy =
vanilla Bubastis and not even think their design problematic.  That's also =
fine, not here to tell you how to live.  I've played games with very new pl=
ayers, where I almost never saw anyone take an optimal move, and they had n=
o issues with Bubastis.  So this is the type of thing that you might only c=
are about after you've played this game a lot.  Either way, if you'd like a=
nother version of Bubastis to play around with, you can try out this varian=
t that is a very simple thing to implement.   Like Tcho-Tcho Tribes, this g=
ives you options if nothing else.<br><br>This change is very easy to implem=
ent in your games because it only has 2 Spellbooks that are swapped; and a =
minor errata change to the Moon.  <br><br>Catabolism gets replaced with Car=
nivore:  <br><br><gg-markup-geekimage><!----><gg-geekimage-ui class=3D"tw-b=
lock"><a href=3D"https://boardgamegeek.com/image/7123210" target=3D"_self">=
<gg-image _nghost-ng-c2249515928=3D""><img _ngcontent-ng-c2249515928=3D"" i=
temprop=3D"image" itemtype=3D"https://schema.org/URL" loading=3D"lazy" clas=
s=3D"img-fluid" src=3D"https://cf.geekdo-images.com/jRi9jZVhVatDV71IITNpmg_=
_medium/img/qNla7HE1kca2oljdBAK6Pucb5_w=3D/fit-in/500x500/filters:no_upscal=
e():strip_icc()/pic7123210.png" alt=3D"no caption" content=3D"https://cf.ge=
ekdo-images.com/jRi9jZVhVatDV71IITNpmg__medium/img/qNla7HE1kca2oljdBAK6Pucb=
5_w=3D/fit-in/500x500/filters:no_upscale():strip_icc()/pic7123210.png" heig=
ht=3D"311" width=3D"500" style=3D"display: inherit;"><!----></gg-image><!--=
--></a><!----><!----><!----></gg-geekimage-ui><!----></gg-markup-geekimage>=
<br><br>Ailurophobia gets replaced with Syzygy: <br><br><gg-markup-geekimag=
e><!----><gg-geekimage-ui class=3D"tw-block"><a href=3D"https://boardgamege=
ek.com/image/7123211" target=3D"_self"><gg-image _nghost-ng-c2249515928=3D"=
"><img _ngcontent-ng-c2249515928=3D"" itemprop=3D"image" itemtype=3D"https:=
//schema.org/URL" loading=3D"lazy" class=3D"img-fluid" src=3D"https://cf.ge=
ekdo-images.com/OnC9kJkTPtgQQXdP9S1jZg__medium/img/fUDAD9igVOAX0QoyUjC4ubZn=
3YI=3D/fit-in/500x500/filters:no_upscale():strip_icc()/pic7123211.png" alt=
=3D"no caption" content=3D"https://cf.geekdo-images.com/OnC9kJkTPtgQQXdP9S1=
jZg__medium/img/fUDAD9igVOAX0QoyUjC4ubZn3YI=3D/fit-in/500x500/filters:no_up=
scale():strip_icc()/pic7123211.png" height=3D"310" width=3D"500" style=3D"d=
isplay: inherit;"><!----></gg-image><!----></a><!----><!----><!----></gg-ge=
ekimage-ui><!----></gg-markup-geekimage><br><br>We have had Bubastis on the=
 TTS (Tabletop Simulator mod) for well over 2 years and well... they seldom=
 get played.  They seldom get played because they have a pretty defined met=
agame that is incredibly warping to the rest of the game, much more so than=
 any other faction (including The Invasion, which is saying a lot) and they=
 are generally very unfun to play against.  The game is an area control gam=
e in which Areas with Gates are the primary thing fought over.  Bubastis si=
desteps this core gameplay aspect by only having 1 Gate, that they can neve=
r lose control of: the Moon.  Bubastis has a reason to interact with you, b=
ut you have very little to zero reason to interact with Bubastis, making it=
 a rather one-sided affair.  Ailurophobia forces Cats to cohabitate with en=
emy factions, but this is pretty meta defined based on what factions are be=
ing played:  <br><br>Great Cthulhu makes a bad 'host' because of Devour and=
 his SBRs (Spellbook Requirements) require him to Kill/Devour enemy Units.<=
br>Crawling Chaos makes a semi-optimal host because of Abduct and Nyarletho=
tep rolling a lot of dice, Emissary and Madness. <br>Black Goat can be a go=
od or bad host depending on if other factions (Madness anyone?) are in play=
, they can use early combats with Frenzy to get into additional Areas easy.=
  <br>Yellow Sign, since they want to get KiY and Hastur out early, make fo=
r a GREAT target for Bubastis to victimize and cohabitate with.  <br>Ancien=
ts would rather not attack and get Pained out of their Area, so they make a=
n ideal target to cohabitate with.  <br>Opener, with Million Favored Ones, =
make a horrible target to cohabitate with because they just get free upgrad=
es constantly.  <br>Windwalker generally can dodge Cat-cohabitation by use =
of Ice Age, Howl, and Arctic Wind.  <br>Daemon Sultan can dodge Ailurophobi=
a by not starting on the board and generally having a pretty diffuse Unit s=
pread until the mid-game.  <br>The Invasion can dodge Ailurophobia by use o=
f Hellgate and Eternal Servitude and not needing to have Units to control t=
heir native Gates (Lord's Shadows).  <br>Sleeper makes for a bad Ailurophob=
ia target because of Capture Monster.  <br><br>This isn't exhaustive, but a=
s you can see, the utility of Ailurophobia is pretty well meta-defined and =
not based on player agency (other than what Faction they are playing).  Thi=
s doesn't result in the best outcomes if all players are playing a proficie=
nt/optimal level.  This is why Ailurophobia is on the chopping block for th=
is variant.  With Ailurophobia, the Bubastis player's optimal play is to pi=
ck on the player that is already losing, since vanilla Bubastis has the fas=
test Doom tempo in the game by quite a bit, they will sail to victory while=
 the rest of the table is pressuring the player that is already losing to d=
o something about all the Cats that are his/her Area.  This makes for a rat=
her unfun, griefy experience.  Indeed, I've seen this exact scenario dozens=
 of times, and drive players from playing CW entirely.  <br><br>The other c=
ore design problem with vanilla Bubastis is Catabolism.  Catabolism basical=
ly sidesteps how cool the Moon is as a concept.  You have a Gate/Area hybri=
d that is adjacent to every map Area, has a 'turnstyle' effect where Factio=
ns can leave the Moon, but can't enter unless you send them there, and you =
get paid Power when they leave.  This very interesting concept is nearly co=
mpletely undermined by Catabolism that gets around needing to Summon your U=
nits to this same Area.  Catabolism is also incredibly accelerating Bubasti=
s tempo to 30 Doom and how rapidly they can achieve their SBRs.  Removing C=
atabolism makes their Moon design-space shine much more brightly as when an=
d how to Catnap enemies, Summon your Cats, and move them from the Moon all =
become multi-dimensional decisions. <br><br>However, if you remove Cataboli=
sm, then Bubastis can only Summon Units to the Moon.  This would make it so=
 that if the Moon ever got Ice-Aged then they would be in an auto-lose situ=
ation.  For this reason (in addition to weird Cathedral interactions) we er=
rata the Moon so that no tokens, buildings and other markers can be placed =
on it.  After all, Ice Aging the Moon is a bit strange thematically as it h=
as no weather.  =3D] <br><br><gg-markup-geekimage><!----><gg-geekimage-ui c=
lass=3D"tw-block"><a href=3D"https://boardgamegeek.com/image/7123213" targe=
t=3D"_self"><gg-image _nghost-ng-c2249515928=3D""><img _ngcontent-ng-c22495=
15928=3D"" itemprop=3D"image" itemtype=3D"https://schema.org/URL" loading=
=3D"lazy" class=3D"img-fluid" src=3D"https://cf.geekdo-images.com/1iMt5yRJ2=
ocGaVxPIhSYvg__large/img/OdtJrD-W9N7DTomJ4SyS5HV_gH4=3D/fit-in/1024x1024/fi=
lters:no_upscale():strip_icc()/pic7123213.png" alt=3D"no caption" content=
=3D"https://cf.geekdo-images.com/1iMt5yRJ2ocGaVxPIhSYvg__large/img/OdtJrD-W=
9N7DTomJ4SyS5HV_gH4=3D/fit-in/1024x1024/filters:no_upscale():strip_icc()/pi=
c7123213.png" height=3D"878" width=3D"1024" style=3D"display: inherit;"><!-=
---></gg-image><!----></a><!----><!----><!----></gg-geekimage-ui><!----></g=
g-markup-geekimage><br><br>You'll notice that this change is so minor that =
you really don't need to print it out, if you're playing with Windwalker, A=
ncients, or Yellow Sign, they can't put their buildings, tokens, etc. on th=
e Moon.  Simple.  <br><br>-------------------------------------------<br><b=
r>With this variant Bubastis' "grief level" is GREATLY diminished.  No long=
er do you have meta-defined matchups in which the most optimal move for Bub=
astis is to swarm a single player, based solely on what faction they are pl=
aying and then yield rapid Doom from Ailurophobia.  No longer do you try an=
d victimize the player that is already losing.  No longer does the table tr=
y and pressure the already losing player to punch Cats rather than play the=
 game.  No longer is the game basically defined on rolling a bunch of Kills=
 to murder a bunch of Cats, and by doing so give Bubastis their spellbooks,=
 which feels even worse.  No longer can they sidestep the Moon's design by =
being able to recruit their Monsters as Cultists via Catabolism.  They are =
no longer super-accelerated in Doom and Spellbook tempo and the rest of the=
 players are able to do fairly normal opening for AP1 and AP2 rather than t=
he entire game living in Bubastis' shadow.  <br><br>Playing as Bubastis is =
also much more rewarding, as you've got to stage and plan your spellbook ac=
quisition in a balanced approach with Doom and Power gain.  Vanilla Bubasti=
s plays pretty straightforwardly, where you can get your Cat from Mars and/=
or Uranus Killed off fairly early to obtain spellbooks rapidly.  Not so wit=
h the variant, as you'll need to invest your Power much more carefully as y=
ou have to Summon all of your Units on the Moon, then move them into positi=
on.  Do you go for having an early Power boost by getting as many Cats in p=
lay, or do you go for an empty-Moon approach and try to optimize Syzygy as =
much as possible?  They have a depth of play that is non-existent with Vani=
lla Bubastis.  <br><br>Catnapping also becomes much more important.  With V=
anilla Bubastis, due to their high Power gain and unit placement efficiency=
 with Catabolism you just stall as long as possible, land a "Super Ritual" =
with Bastet and then try and survive with low-20s Doom, putting the onus on=
 the entire table to stop a runaway player.  With this version of Bubastis =
the onus is on YOU to win by carefully managing your Units and placing them=
 for long-term setup for a rapid endgame win using Carnivore.  <br><br>With=
 Vanilla Bubastis you have 3 really cool combat abilities and you have almo=
st no offensive reason to use them.  You gain Doom by cohabitating with ene=
my players, meaning that these abilities are used defensively, which make t=
he grief factor and other player engagement even worse.  Not only do other =
players have to 'jump on the grenade' to prevent a Bubastis win, they have =
to 'charge the machine-gun nest' as they are either attacking into Bubastis=
 that gives them their Spellbooks, or if they already have their spellbooks=
 then they are charging into nasty combat abilities like Zagazig, Predator =
and Savagery.  With the variant, the onus is on the Bubastis player to plan=
 and coordinate their attacks in most optimal way to further their own win =
condition rather than demand the rest of the players to stop Bubastis while=
 gaining nothing for doing so (other than not losing, which is the type of =
1-dimensionality that you'd see all over the place in a game like Root but =
seldom in CW. )<br><br>I hope that you folks have as much with this variant=
 as we have.  No longer do we dread/groan when people play Bubastis, with t=
his variant they are always a welcome addition to the game.  <br><br>With V=
anilla Bubastis, they are so game warping that CW takes on a completely dif=
ferent feel.  Most importantly, with this Bubastis variant, the core gamepl=
ay is intact and CW still <em>feels </em>like CW while still allowing of th=
e central weirdness of the Bubastis faction.  That's what is most important=
 to me about this change.<!----></gg-markup-content></div></gg-markup-quote=
